.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_STREAM_ERROR 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_stream_error \- report why the last chttpsvr_req_read call failed
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_req_stream_error(const chttpsvr_req *req);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Reports the reason the most recent chttpsvr_req_read call on req
returned -1.  Meaningful only immediately after such a -1 return;
otherwise the result is unspecified.

Distinguishes only chttpsvr_req_read's genuine, data-dependent
transfer errors (see chttpsvr_req_read(3)).  It reports ccol_success,
indistinguishable from "no error," when the -1 was instead caused by
caller misuse (buf was NULL with buflen > 0, or the request's route
was not registered with chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler); both
are static programming errors reachable on every call to the
offending code rather than only sometimes, so a caller hitting one is
expected to find and fix it during ordinary testing rather than
diagnose it at runtime.

Return values:
  ccol_timed_out             stream_read_timeout_ms elapsed while
                              waiting for the next batch, or the
                              request's total body read time exceeded
                              max_body_read_duration_ms (if set).
  ccol_msg_too_large         The body exceeded max_body_size
                              mid-stream, or a single chunk's declared
                              size (chunked Transfer-Encoding) exceeded
                              max_body_size before any of that chunk's
                              data was read.
  ccol_not_enough_memory     An internal allocation failed while
                              growing the body buffer; not caused by
                              the client.
  ccol_http_transfer_aborted The connection was closed or reset, or
                              the body's framing was malformed.
  ccol_success               No error is on record for req, or the -1
                              was actually caused by one of the
                              caller-misuse cases above.
  ccol_unexpected_failure    req is NULL or its handle is invalid.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_read (3),
.BR chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler (3)
